  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SCLR 3-Yrs Crisis-affected communities in Palestinian camps are supported to work together to improve their lives</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The SCLR model necessarily entails an initial investment in a smaller number of rights holders, as they gain the experience and skills required to lead and deliver improvements for their communities.&#xa0; The return on this is that the community groups remain established and in a position to continue delivering far beyond the life of the project.&#xa0;&#xa0;&#xa0;


The project aims to reach, at a minimum, 250 people over the three years with at least 50% women directly benefiting from the project .&#xa0; As community leaders, there is a clear multiplier effect, which will be explored through monitoring and evaluation over the course of the project.&#xa0; In addition to community members, the project will also indirectly impact the wider community. Other main stakeholders will include popular committees,16 UNRWA, Palestinian NGOs and community-based associations e.g. sports, scouts, cultural clubs etc. who will be engaged to mobilise the community and spread the word about the project to ensure all different groups are informed and engaged in the project.&#xa0;&#xa0;


In the first year, workshops will be conducted to inform the crisis-affected communities about the project including its objectives, budget and limitations. Posters will be displayed and distributed to advertise the project and invite people to apply. The local community will be informed of safeguarding and complaint mechanisms.&#xa0; According to a recent post-distribution monitoring report (PDM)17 which included a review of preferred feedback and complaints mechanisms, 84% of people knew how to submit feedback or complaints about the project.&#x202f;Most people&#x2019;s preferred mechanism was direct contact, i.e. face to face meetings, and the use of complaint boxes. As an additional mechanism, AN will also activate a&#xa0; hotline number (with WhatsApp) which will be shared with the public.&#xa0;&#xa0;&#xa0;


CA and AN are committed to reaching up to 250 people over the three years and this will be kept under review depending on interest. The distribution of the people reached across the three camps will be dependent on the interest and capacities of those engaged in the initial phase of the project. To ensure involvement of a diverse range of people CA and AN are committed to include at least 60% females, 40% young people, 5% people with disabilities and 5% elderly people.</narrative>
  </description>
